Replaces Far Cry 2's sky, clouds and the light they cast with an atmosphere that follows the sun through the whole day, the weather and the night.

[size=4][b]What it changes[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]The sky[/b] - the engine's painted dome is replaced by a sky worked out from the sun's position: deep blue at noon, orange and purple sunsets, a lingering twilight, and stars that stay out all night. The land's distant fog takes on the sky's own horizon colour.[/*]
[*][b]Volumetric clouds[/b] - a layer of real, lit cumulus with silver linings and dark bases, a high sheet of cirrus above it, and aircraft trails.[/*]
[*][b]A sky that belongs to your save[/b] - the clouds drift with the game's clock, so loading a save shows the sky you left, and the hours you spend (or sleep through) move it on. Every midnight rolls a new set of one to three aircraft trails.[/*]
[*][b]Storms[/b] - the clouds thicken, the cirrus closes over the whole sky and the light goes grey, without the land drowning in grey fog.[/*]
[*][b]The sun[/b] - light shafts that stop where the clouds stand, a glare that washes over the view when you look toward the sun and that clouds cover, and a fading afterimage if you stare at it.[/*]
[*][b]Cloud shadows[/b] - the ground under a cloud loses the sun as it drifts over.[/*]
[*][b]Ambient occlusion[/b] - the corners and creases the light doesn't reach darken, worked out from a depth of the world drawn a second time without its grass, leaves or your own weapon.[/*]
[*][b]The night[/b] - a smaller moon lit on the clouds and no longer hidden by fog, darker blue-grey nights with moonlight that throws shadows once the moon is up, and the world's colour draining where it is dark while fires and lamps keep theirs.[/*]
[*][b]The colour grade[/b] - the game's yellow cast replaced by a neutral grade you can tune.[/*]
[/list]

Each part can be switched back to the engine's own in the [b]Mod Configuration[/b] menu: Sky, Clouds, Sun, Night, Shadows, Occlusion and Grade. Every effect's values sit in [b]bin\sky-overhaul.ini[/b], and with [b]DevTools[/b] installed a window with sliders for all of them opens in its overlay.

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/farcry2/mods/368]FCSE[/url], the plugin loader this runs in. Far Cry 2 1.03, Steam or GOG.[/*]
[*][b]Bloom on[/b] in Options, Video. Without it the sun beside the glare is a flat disc.[/*]
[*][b]Depth pass quality high[/b] or above for the cloud shadows. Below it the rest still works.[/*]
[/list]

[size=4][b]Installing[/b][/size]

The download is a JackAll layer, which both installers read as-is:
[list]
[*][b]Vortex[/b] - with the [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/site/mods/2143]Far Cry 2 extension[/url] installed, drop the zip in and enable it.[/*]
[*][b]JackAll[/b] - add the zip in [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/farcry2/mods/370]JackAll[/url].[/*]
[/list]

[size=4][b]Compatibility[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Lighting, fog and weather mods[/b] - the night lighting ships as the world's whole preset library, and the moon and storm changes as the world's whole environment block, so any other mod that edits either will conflict. Whichever loads last wins.[/*]
[*][b]Other rendering plugins[/b] - FCSE gives each Direct3D hook to one plugin, so a second plugin that draws into the frame the same way will be refused. DevTools and UFCP work alongside it.[/*]
[/list]

[size=4][b]Known issues[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]The night's colour drain also applies indoors.[/*]
[/list]
